---
// Hand-authored inline SVG: how the FSA∩CMA overlap shape is measured by the
// even-odd rule instead of by winding direction. Inline (not an <img>) so it
// inherits the Broadsheet tokens from global.css and needs no client JS.
//
// Unlike RegionGraphNYC/RegionGraphChicago, this diagram encodes an *algorithm*,
// not seed data — there is no TOML to transcribe from. The source of truth is
// polygonArea + nestingDepth in the Atlas repo's
// api/internal/etl/ca/geom.go:
//   depth := number of other contours containing this one
//   depth % 2 == 0  ->  area added        (filled)
//   depth % 2 == 1  ->  area subtracted   (hole)
// The shape is deliberately drawn as ONE <path> with fill-rule="evenodd", so
// the browser fills it by the same rule the ETL measures it by — the picture
// and the code agree by construction rather than by my redrawing them to match.
//
// A and B are drawn wound the SAME direction on purpose: that is the bug the
// passage describes, and the reason winding can't be the signal.
//
// Styles live in global.css (.post-diagram, .dg-*) per the one-stylesheet rule.
// Marker and aria IDs are prefixed dg-area-* — three diagrams share this page.
---

<figure class="post-figure post-diagram">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 680 400"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="dg-area-title dg-area-desc"
    class="dg"
  >
    <title id="dg-area-title">Measuring an overlap shape by nesting depth</title>
    <desc id="dg-area-desc">
      On the left, an irregular overlap shape made of three nested outlines:
      A, the outer boundary; B, a hole inside A; and C, an island inside B.
      Arrows on A and B show both are drawn in the same direction, so the
      direction an outline is drawn in cannot distinguish a hole from a
      boundary. On the right, the rule that does work: A sits inside zero
      other outlines, an even number, so its area is added. B sits inside one
      outline, an odd number, so its area is subtracted. C sits inside two,
      an even number, so its area is added.
    </desc>

    <defs>
      <marker
        id="dg-area-arrow"
        viewBox="0 0 10 10"
        refX="9"
        refY="5"
        markerWidth="6"
        markerHeight="6"
        orient="auto"
      >
        <path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" class="dg-arrowhead" />
      </marker>
    </defs>

    {/* ---- the overlap shape: one path, three subpaths, even-odd fill ----
         Rendered by the browser exactly as polygonArea sums it. */}
    <path
      class="dg-poly"
      fill-rule="evenodd"
      d="M 55 305 L 40 195 L 85 110 L 165 62 L 285 60 L 360 105 L 385 195 L 355 285 L 265 335 L 145 330 Z
         M 130 240 L 118 172 L 165 128 L 250 122 L 300 170 L 288 245 L 205 275 Z
         M 185 225 L 178 190 L 210 172 L 245 195 L 232 232 Z"
    />

    {/* ---- each outline stroked separately so it can be pointed at ---- */}
    <path
      class="dg-ring"
      d="M 55 305 L 40 195 L 85 110 L 165 62 L 285 60 L 360 105 L 385 195 L 355 285 L 265 335 L 145 330 Z"
    />
    <path
      class="dg-ring dg-ring--hole"
      d="M 130 240 L 118 172 L 165 128 L 250 122 L 300 170 L 288 245 L 205 275 Z"
    />
    <path
      class="dg-ring"
      d="M 185 225 L 178 190 L 210 172 L 245 195 L 232 232 Z"
    />

    {/* ---- winding arrows: A and B point the same way, which is the point ---- */}
    <g class="dg-wind" fill="none" marker-end="url(#dg-area-arrow)">
      <path d="M 195 61.5 H 250" />
      <path d="M 180 127 L 232 123" />
    </g>

    {/* ---- outline badges ---- */}
    <g>
      <circle cx="40" cy="195" r="11" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="40" y="200" class="dg-label">A</text>
    </g>
    <g>
      <circle cx="118" cy="172" r="11" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="118" y="177" class="dg-label">B</text>
    </g>
    <g>
      <circle cx="178" cy="190" r="11" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="178" y="195" class="dg-label">C</text>
    </g>

    <text x="212" y="372" class="dg-note">
      A and B are wound the same way — direction can't tell them apart
    </text>

    {/* ---- the rule that does work ---- */}
    <text x="425" y="78" class="dg-note dg-note--start">
      so instead, count what each one sits inside:
    </text>

    <g>
      <circle cx="440" cy="120" r="11" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="440" y="125" class="dg-label">A</text>
      <text x="462" y="116" class="dg-row">inside 0 outlines</text>
      <text x="462" y="133" class="dg-call dg-call--start">even → added</text>
    </g>

    <g>
      <circle cx="440" cy="195" r="11" class="dg-node dg-node--anchor" />
      <text x="440" y="200" class="dg-label">B</text>
      <text x="462" y="191" class="dg-row">inside 1 outline — A</text>
      <text x="462" y="208" class="dg-call dg-call--start">odd → subtracted</text>
    </g>

    <g>
      <circle cx="440" cy="270" r="11" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="440" y="275" class="dg-label">C</text>
      <text x="462" y="266" class="dg-row">inside 2 outlines — A and B</text>
      <text x="462" y="283" class="dg-call dg-call--start">even → added</text>
    </g>
  </svg>

  <figcaption>
    The shape on the left is drawn as a single path under the even-odd rule, so
    the browser fills it the same way the pipeline measures it: A is solid, B is
    the hole inside A, C is an island inside B. Counting nesting rather than
    reading winding direction is what makes the answer independent of how the
    clipping library happened to draw each outline.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
